- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) might be giants, but apparent magnitude depends on distance, so it's not a strong direct indicator alone.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer stars. Giants tend to be more luminous and can be seen farther away, so stars with small parallax but bright absolute magnitude might be giants.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude estimation, making classification harder.
- B-V (color index): Giants often have distinct color indices, typically redder (higher B-V) for cooler giants, but this depends on spectral type.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III for giants, IV for subgiants, V for main sequence dwarfs) is a direct indicator of giant status.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower absolute magnitude (are more luminous) than dwarfs of the same spectral type, so a lower Amag is indicative of giants.

